const schema = [{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"Service","name":"Clinical trial support","serviceType":"Research service","provider":{"@type":"Organization","name":"Pubrica","url":"https://pubrica.com/"},"url":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/product-development/clinical-trial-support/","description":"Protocol, IB, ICF, SAP and CSR authoring, independent analysis, registry disclosure and inspection-readiness review — built to ICH E6(R3), M11, E3 and.","areaServed":"Worldwide","audience":{"@type":"Audience","audienceType":"Researchers, PhD scholars, research institutions, medical writers and life-science teams"}},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are you a CRO?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No, and the distinction is legal rather than presentational. In the United States, 21 CFR 312.3 defines a contract research organisation as a party that assumes one or more sponsor obligations, and 312.52 provides that a CRO which does so is subject to the same regulatory action as a sponsor. We accept no such transfer.\n\nIn India the line is harder still: since 1 April 2025, Chapter VA of the amended New Drugs and Clinical Trials Rules makes CDSCO registration mandatory for any organisation conducting trials or bioequivalence studies on a sponsor’s behalf. We produce documentation, analysis and evidence; you remain the sponsor and hold every obligation."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Then what happens to the parts you do not do?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"They go to a contracted CRO, a site management organisation or your own team, and we work alongside them. Monitoring, site management, patient recruitment, investigational product handling and safety case processing of record all sit there.\n\nThis is a normal division of labour rather than an unusual one. Several substantial firms in this market sell exactly the documentation and evidence scope without trial conduct, and sponsors routinely buy the two separately."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why does the ICH version matter so much?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because several of them changed recently and most material in circulation has not caught up. E6(R3) replaced E6(R2), with Annex 1 effective in the EU in July 2025 and Annex 2 from January 2027. M11 became the harmonised protocol standard at Step 4 in November 2025. CONSORT and SPIRIT both moved to 2025 versions, the Declaration of Helsinki was revised in 2024, and GPP 2022 replaced GPP3.\n\nFor device sponsors the sharpest one is ISO 14155: the 2020 edition is withdrawn and the fourth edition was published in March 2026. A clinical investigation plan written to the withdrawn version is a finding waiting to happen."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can you help with a trial that is already running?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and it is a common point of entry. Amendments, interim reports, DSUR and annual reports, a results record that is approaching or past its deadline, or a CSR for a trial that finished some time ago.\n\nIf the trial is mid-flight the first thing worth doing is usually a disclosure check, because those deadlines run whether or not anybody is watching them, and FDA has moved from pre-notice letters to notices of noncompliance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Who signs and submits the documents?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"You do. Registry records are drafted and filed on your account under your responsible party; regulatory content is prepared for your signature; the protocol and the CSR carry your name.\n\nThis is not a formality. Obligations that are not transferred in writing remain with the sponsor, and an oversight chain that is unclear about who holds what is itself something inspectors record."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you do the statistics, or only the writing?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Both. Sample size and power, randomisation schedule specification, the analysis plan with the estimand framed under E9(R1), and independent analysis against your locked dataset with tables, listings and figures.\n\nWhat we do not do is own the clinical database. Database build, validation, lock and custody as the system of record sit with your data management function or your CRO — and we analyse what they lock."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What does the free amendment risk check actually look at?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Eligibility criteria against the population each one excludes; endpoints against whether an estimand exists for them; the schedule of assessments against which procedures map to an objective; and the registration and disclosure obligations the geography creates.\n\nThose four account for most avoidable amendments. The 2016 Tufts work found 45% of amendments avoidable, and the more recent data shows the count rising rather than falling."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you work on device and supplement studies as well as drug trials?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. Device studies are built to ISO 14155:2026, 21 CFR 812 and EU MDR rather than to ICH E6 alone, with the significant-risk determination driving whether a full IDE is needed.\n\nFor supplement and nutraceutical studies the prior question is whether an IND is required, which turns on the claim under study rather than on the product: a structure-function claim generally does not trigger one, a disease claim generally does. We will tell you which side of that line your protocol is on before you design around it."}}]},{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://pubrica.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"Research services","item":"https://pubrica.com/services/research-services/product-development/clinical-trial-support/"}]}];

